## Why This Is Asked
Explain flag-driven, safe rollouts across CNPA boundaries.

## Key Concepts
- Feature flags, schema evolution, backward/forward compatibility, idempotent writes.
- Outbox pattern, nullable fields, partial rollouts.
- Observability: metrics for latency, error, cohort analytics.

## Code Example
```javascript
// Node: read flag, set tenant_id only for flagged users
if (flagEnabled(user)) {
  payload.tenant_id = user.tenantId
} else {
  payload.tenant_id = null
}
// write to DB and enqueue to Kafka with the same payload
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How to handle schema migrations without downtime?
- How to test rollback in CI/CD?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to implement cross-service consistency guarantees and reason about end-to-end latency under load.

## Key Concepts

- transactional outbox across DB and event bus
- idempotent producers and deduplication
- cache invalidation vs cache warmth
- end-to-end validation under peak load

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo-code: transactional outbox pattern
async function commitUpdate(userId, pref) {
  await db.begin();
  await db.run('UPDATE users SET pref=? WHERE id=?', pref, userId);
  await db.run('INSERT INTO outbox (user_id, event_type, payload, processed) VALUES (?,?,?,false)', userId, 'pref_updated', JSON.stringify({pref}));
  await kafkaProducer.send({ topic: 'user.events', key: String(userId), value: JSON.stringify({userId, pref, ts: Date.now()}) });
  await db.commit();
  await cache.invalidate(`user:${userId}:pref`);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you test late-arriving events?
- How do you monitor and alert on read-after-write latency violations?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests practical comfort with distributed tracing in CNPA stacks; covers instrumentation, propagation, sampling, and verification under load.

## Key Concepts

- OpenTelemetry instrumentation across HTTP, DB, Kafka, Redis
- Trace context propagation (W3C vs B3)
- Spans, attributes, and correlation IDs
- Sampling impact and overhead

## Code Example

```javascript
// minimal end-to-end tracing snippet
const tracer = require('@opentelemetry/api').trace.getTracer('cnpa-service');
app.get('/register', (req, res) => {
  const span = tracer.startSpan('http_request');
  // propagate context to downstream
  // DB insert and Kafka publish here
  span.end();
  res.end('ok');
});
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you handle high-cardinality attributes in traces?
- How would you test end-to-end traces under load without affecting prod?

## Why This Is Asked

End-to-end visibility across services helps beginners diagnose where causality breaks in CNPA pipelines, a common production issue after deployments.

## Key Concepts

- Distributed tracing across HTTP, DB, and messaging layers
- Trace propagation (W3C traceparent) and context maintenance
- Sampling strategies and exporters (OpenTelemetry, Jaeger/OTLP)
- End-to-end verification with test injections and trace reconciliation

## Code Example

```javascript
// Example using OpenTelemetry in Node.js
const { trace } = require('@opentelemetry/api');
const tracer = trace.getTracer('cnpa-api');
async function handle(req, res){
  const span = tracer.startSpan('http_handler');
  // perform DB insert and Kafka publish here
  span.end();
  res.end('ok');
